<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Failaka Island</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Failaka</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Failaka is an island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuwait">Kuwait</a> City. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> it was an outpost of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dilmun">Dilmun</a> culture, with settlements, temples and seals of Dilmun type, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a> it held a fortress and temples under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a>, when it was known as Ikaros. It has been largely uninhabited since the Iraqi invasion of 1990.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/failaka-island.jpg" alt="Failaka Island" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ரோஜா · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beach_at_Failaka_Island.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Failaka lies in the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, 20 kilometres off the coast of Kuwait City and about 50 kilometres south-east of the point where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> empty into the Gulf. For thousands of years it was a strategic prize in the trade that passed up and down the Gulf. The name Failaka is thought to derive from the Greek phylakion, meaning outpost. Its village of Al-Zawr, on the north-west side, was the longest continuously inhabited place in Kuwait until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-war">Gulf War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Mesopotamian traders from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> settled the island around 2000 BCE, and its buildings of that time resemble those found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. Sumerian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> texts found on the island name it Agarum, the land of the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inzak">Enzak</a>, and it became a hub of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dilmun">Dilmun</a> civilisation. A seal of about 1650 BCE preserves the name of a king's daughter, and the title servant of Inzak of Akarum was the king's title in Dilmun. Failaka was later under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassite-dynasty">Kassite dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, and in the Neo-Babylonian period <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a> had a palace and temple there, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus">Nabonidus</a> had a governor.</p><p>After an apparent abandonment of about seven centuries, the bay of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuwait">Kuwait</a> was resettled in the Achaemenid period. In 324 BCE Alexander the Great sent expeditions to explore the Gulf, and the island was named Ikaros, because, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a>, it resembled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> island of that name. Remains include a large Hellenistic fort and two Greek temples; Strabo wrote of a temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> and an oracle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artemis">Artemis</a>. Later a commercial station of the kingdom of Characene existed there. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Nestorian Christian</a> farms, villages and two large churches of the fifth and sixth centuries flourished until the ninth century.</p><p>A Danish team under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geoffrey-bibby">Geoffrey Bibby</a> excavated four tells between 1958 and 1963 and found about 50 cylinder seals and about 400 stamp seals, most of the stamp seals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dilmun">Dilmun</a> type. French, Italian, Polish, Slovak and Greek teams have worked there since, as have groups from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Before the Iraqi invasion of 1990 the island had over two thousand residents and several schools. During 1990 and 1991 the invading Iraqis expelled all its residents to the mainland and mined the beaches. After the war the island was cleared of mines, though it remains under some military use. Many old homes still stood empty, and a resort has made it a holiday destination for visitors from Kuwait City.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Failaka Island” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Failaka Island</b> off Kuwait, with its Dilmun-period temple and seal workshop, was part of the same political and commercial sphere.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dilmun">Dilmun · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
